## Why `registerBeanDefinition` throws `BeanDefinitionOverrideException`

In `DefaultListableBeanFactory.registerBeanDefinition` (`spring-beans/…/DefaultListableBeanFactory.java:1252-1277`):

```java
BeanDefinition existingDefinition = this.beanDefinitionMap.get(beanName);
if (existingDefinition != null) {
    if (!isBeanDefinitionOverridable(beanName)) {
        throw new BeanDefinitionOverrideException(beanName, beanDefinition, existingDefinition);
    }
    else {
        logBeanDefinitionOverriding(beanName, beanDefinition, existingDefinition);
    }
    this.beanDefinitionMap.put(beanName, beanDefinition);
}
```

The same check is repeated for the alias case a few lines below (`:1279-1291`) when a new definition is registered under a name that's currently an alias for an existing definition.

So the exception fires when a bean definition is registered under a `beanName` that already has a definition **and** overriding is not allowed for that name.

## Under what configuration

`isBeanDefinitionOverridable` delegates to `isAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding()` (`:1447-1449`), which is driven by the `allowBeanDefinitionOverriding` field:

```java
private @Nullable Boolean allowBeanDefinitionOverriding;
...
public boolean isAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding() {
    return !Boolean.FALSE.equals(this.allowBeanDefinitionOverriding);
}
```

- Default value is `null` → `isAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding()` returns `true` → duplicate registrations silently **replace** the old definition (with an INFO/WARN log via `logBeanDefinitionOverriding`), no exception.
- The exception only happens when someone explicitly calls `setAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding(false)` on the factory (`:270-272`), and a second bean definition is later registered under a name already taken.

In practice this configuration is most commonly hit via **Spring Boot**, which sets `spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=false` by default. So the typical trigger is: two `@Configuration` classes (or an XML file and a `@Bean` method, or a component-scanned class) both defining a bean with the same name/id, e.g. two `@Bean` methods named `dataSource()` in different configs, or a `@Component` class whose generated name collides with an existing bean.

## How applications commonly avoid it

- Give conflicting beans distinct names (`@Bean("myDataSource")`) or `@Qualifier`s instead of relying on identical default names.
- Use `@Primary`/`@ConditionalOnMissingBean` (Boot autoconfiguration pattern) so only one definition wins deterministically instead of overriding by name collision.
- If overriding is intentional (e.g., test configuration replacing a production bean), explicitly opt back in:
  - Boot property: `spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=true`
  - Plain Spring: `((DefaultListableBeanFactory) beanFactory).setAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding(true)`, or `GenericApplicationContext.setAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding(true)` before refresh.
- Avoid duplicate component scans / imports of the same `@Configuration` that would register the same bean name twice.

