## Why it throws

In `DefaultListableBeanFactory.registerBeanDefinition()` (`spring-beans/.../support/DefaultListableBeanFactory.java:1268-1276`), if a bean definition already exists under the same name, Spring checks `isBeanDefinitionOverridable(beanName)`. If that returns `false`, it throws `BeanDefinitionOverrideException` instead of silently replacing the definition:

```java
BeanDefinition existingDefinition = this.beanDefinitionMap.get(beanName);
if (existingDefinition != null) {
    if (!isBeanDefinitionOverridable(beanName)) {
        throw new BeanDefinitionOverrideException(beanName, beanDefinition, existingDefinition);
    }
    else {
        logBeanDefinitionOverriding(beanName, beanDefinition, existingDefinition);
    }
    this.beanDefinitionMap.put(beanName, beanDefinition);
}
```

The same check applies when a new bean definition is registered under a name that's currently used as an *alias* for another bean (lines 1279-1291) — overridability is checked there too.

## Configuration that triggers it

`isBeanDefinitionOverridable()` (line 1447) delegates to `isAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding()` (line 280), which is backed by the `allowBeanDefinitionOverriding` flag (default `null`, i.e. `true`-equivalent — `!Boolean.FALSE.equals(...)`). This is set via `setAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding(false)` on the factory. So the exception happens when:
- `allowBeanDefinitionOverriding` has been explicitly set to `false` (e.g. Spring Boot sets this via `spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=false`, which is Boot's default since 2.1), **and**
- two bean definitions are registered under the same name (or a name colliding with an existing alias).

## How applications commonly avoid it

- Give beans distinct names (most common fix — an accidental name clash, e.g. two `@Bean` methods or a component-scanned class and an XML/`@Bean` definition sharing a name).
- Explicitly enable overriding: Spring Boot property `spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=true`, or programmatically `((DefaultListableBeanFactory) beanFactory).setAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding(true)`.
- Use `@Primary`/`@Qualifier` for legitimate multi-bean-of-same-type scenarios instead of relying on name-based override, since overriding is really meant for "replace this exact bean" use cases (e.g. test configuration swapping out a production bean).
